WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.829
So now let's go to the next layer of the

00:00:01.839 --> 00:00:03.990
framework which is memory. Now memory

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.150
simply put is everything that cla knows

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:07.990
about you and your work and just as

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.870
importantly how fast and how cheaply it

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.470
can retrieve it because the more that

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.390
you use your agents the more files

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.470
eventually pile up and searching all of

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.870
them every time can be slow and can burn

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.790
through your usage. And in my view when

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.390
we talk about memory you can split it

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.470
into three levels. And the first level

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.950
is long-term memory which is essentially

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.790
everything that is saved in your

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.029
workspace. And when people say second

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:32.870
brain systems nowadays, that's really

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.110
just a fancy term for those files and

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:36.950
those data that is contained in your

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.549
workspace, which is probably just a

00:00:38.559 --> 00:00:40.549
folder in your computer. Now, my second

00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:42.549
brain system, or more specifically, the

00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:44.790
visual to my second brain system would

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:46.389
be this. And from here, you can see all

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:47.750
of the applications I have cloud

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.229
connected to, my routines, my individual

00:00:50.239 --> 00:00:52.869
files split by departments, and also the

00:00:52.879 --> 00:00:54.869
skills there in the center. That is all

00:00:54.879 --> 00:00:57.029
routed to by my claw.md here in the

00:00:57.039 --> 00:00:58.470
center. Now, to be clear, you don't

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.630
really need a fancy map or visual to

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.310
have an effective second brain system.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:03.990
Really, in fact, this is useful to me

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.670
because I do a lot of AI education and

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.510
training. So, if I need to show my files

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.030
and skills like what I've been doing in

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.390
this video, it's just much more

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.310
understandable and also faster for me to

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.630
search for them here versus, let's say,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.030
I'm using my normal file explorer, which

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.190
is just slower and is less engaging. And

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.630
this folder, by the way, that is the

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.550
exact same files and folders that are

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.310
represented here also in this map. So,

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.990
if you want a nice visual, if it's

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.670
relevant for your work or you think it's

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.350
better or a bit more fun, then go ahead.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:30.710
Because honestly, with today's models,

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:31.990
Claude can create something like this

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.069
for you pretty quickly. But I'd argue

00:01:34.079 --> 00:01:36.550
that the main value of all second brain

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.390
systems really is if it gives you either

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.550
or both of things. Either you have a

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.469
system that gives you a faster speed of

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:44.870
retrieval or a cheaper cost of

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.109
retrieval. Or simply put, can Claude

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:49.190
quickly find your stuff without burning

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.429
through your usage limits, especially as

00:01:51.439 --> 00:01:53.190
you use agents more and more. You can

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.429
see my workspace here has something like

00:01:55.439 --> 00:01:58.389
420,000 files as of the moment. So you

00:01:58.399 --> 00:01:59.749
definitely would need to have a system

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:01.990
of retrieval for this if it balloons to

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.830
this size. And from what I've found

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.749
really my setup in my system here is

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:07.749
quite simple, at least simpler than most

00:02:07.759 --> 00:02:09.270
because there's really just three layers

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.029
here that I use. And so I'll go through

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:12.710
them and see if that works for you as

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.470
well because I just find that even with

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:16.550
that big workspace, just having these

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.790
three simple layers of long-term memory

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.470
is already enough for me. And the core

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.869
idea that cuts across all these three is

00:02:22.879 --> 00:02:24.949
that we are basically just giving claude

00:02:24.959 --> 00:02:27.510
a map of our entire second brain or

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.190
workspace. So for example, the very

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.270
first layer here which is default for a

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.150
lot of claude code is memory.md. Now

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.390
memory md by default claude would

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.390
already keep this file for you and by

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:39.670
default it would just give you one short

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.509
line for each thing it remembers about

00:02:41.519 --> 00:02:43.670
you. And how cloud code is set up is

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.830
that it actually loads this file for you

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.910
at the start of every chat. So claude

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.070
has already read through this memory. MD

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:51.830
when you start your first prompt. So you

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.910
can think of it like a libraries card

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:55.670
catalog for example where instead of CL

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.990
having to find and walk every shelf it

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.070
would check the catalog first for a map

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:01.990
of your entire library. And you can see

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.030
for my workspace this is my memory MD.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.350
And the reason why it is connected to

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.430
all of these other nodes across my

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.270
different departments like in content in

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.589
community and so on is because what I'm

00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:15.270
trying to do is take advantage of the

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.270
fact that it gets preloaded on every

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.270
session and so that way it has linkages

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.030
to each and every part of my workspace

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.869
which just helps Claude navigate the

00:03:22.879 --> 00:03:24.710
entire map better. Now for a small

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.470
starting workspace or second brain that

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.070
is usually good enough. But as your

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.509
workspace grows and you're finding that

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:31.670
claude is having difficulty retrieving

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.509
information for you, what you can do is

00:03:33.519 --> 00:03:35.190
to use the next layer which is your

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.589
cla.md. Cloud.md that is a file of

00:03:37.599 --> 00:03:39.190
instructions that claude reads at the

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.710
start of every chat. And although the

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.110
previous guideline to using claw.md is

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:45.270
to set it up as a sort of a handbook of

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.509
how things run within your workspace, I

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:49.430
actually set mine up as a map of the

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:50.869
whole workspace of the entire second

00:03:50.879 --> 00:03:52.550
brain. And the reason why I have it here

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.470
in the center is because I actually have

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.710
it route to the different departments of

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.470
my work. Like if we're operating on

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.309
content, then these are the files that

00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:02.070
it can work on mainly. If we're

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.990
operating on personal stuff, then these

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.830
are those files. And by the way, just to

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.509
mention, cloud code actually now reads

00:04:07.519 --> 00:04:10.149
agents.m MD2. So if your workspace

00:04:10.159 --> 00:04:12.309
already has one for other AI tools like

00:04:12.319 --> 00:04:14.149
Codex, then you can just have one

00:04:14.159 --> 00:04:16.870
central agent.md as your core operating

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:18.469
rules. Now, like I said, as part of my

00:04:18.479 --> 00:04:20.870
cloud.MD, MD I have there a map of my

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.870
whole workspace. Then what you can do is

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:24.710
have each department have its own file

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.550
that routes against. So for example, I

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.790
have a business.m MD that routes to the

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.030
subfiles and folders within it. And I

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:32.629
find that that is usually enough when

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:34.550
working with agents because today's 5.5

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.310
models are really smart enough in order

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.390
to just use plain text to find what it

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:39.749
needs. So if you're more advanced, you

00:04:39.759 --> 00:04:41.350
can see I'm not even using any vector

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:43.670
embeddings, which I may do that if I

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.590
feel some sort of pain with retrieval,

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.990
but as of now it works for me. my usage

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.510
limits don't get drained up whenever I

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:51.510
use cloud and so there's no cost to

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.909
overhaul or upgrade this system at least

00:04:53.919 --> 00:04:55.590
as of yet and in fact just to mention

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.749
Boris Jurnney the creator of claude code

00:04:57.759 --> 00:04:59.830
and and who leads the team now he also

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.590
mentioned that even in their workspace

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.270
so this is entropic with probably

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:05.030
millions of files already they're also

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.430
finding that plain text is much better

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.909
versus previous complex setups of vector

00:05:09.919 --> 00:05:11.990
embeddings and rag databases what was

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.150
popular just a while back so if it works

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:15.909
for them then most likely it will work

00:05:15.919 --> 00:05:17.510
for your business too And the third

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.510
layer which is just a supplementary is

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:21.670
obviously skills because as we mentioned

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:23.749
earlier you can actually have a database

00:05:23.759 --> 00:05:26.230
skills right so these database skills it

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:28.230
just tells claude where your information

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.550
lives so you can literally have a skill

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.270
called slashname of business then data

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:34.870
and that skill whenever you reference it

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:36.790
in the chat that just points to where

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:38.469
the up-to-date information or even

00:05:38.479 --> 00:05:40.230
dashboards of that particular business

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:42.950
or client is in and the skill.md of

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.950
these database skills they also adopt

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:46.230
the same principle where they are

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.230
routers so that when Claude reads them,

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:50.070
it can actually infer where to find the

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:51.909
information you're asking for depending

00:05:51.919 --> 00:05:53.670
on your prompt. But the point being that

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:55.510
you need to utilize each of these three

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.350
layers and just make your whole second

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:58.710
brain searchable. That's number one.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:00.230
Plain text turned out to work really

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.310
well for these LLMs. And if you have a

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:04.070
lot of metadata, especially for visual

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:05.909
assets that just makes it easier for

00:06:05.919 --> 00:06:07.990
your agent to search through your entire

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.430
second brain. And if you need a prompt

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.189
to get started with everything I covered

00:06:11.199 --> 00:06:12.950
just now, then you can try it out using

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:14.629
this one, which I also included in that

00:06:14.639 --> 00:06:16.150
guide below. Now, if level one is

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.629
long-term memory, level two is something

00:06:18.639 --> 00:06:20.550
that I often don't see being talked

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:22.550
about as much, and it is Claude's

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.870
short-term memory, which is what Claude

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:27.270
can see in your chat to it in that

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:28.629
particular session. Now, technically,

00:06:28.639 --> 00:06:29.830
the short-term memory is called the

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.670
context window, which is essentially

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.430
Claude's working memory for this one

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.510
conversation. And the new Cloud 5.5

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.430
models, they have a 1 million token

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.189
context window. And just to put that in

00:06:39.199 --> 00:06:41.350
perspective, that's about the first four

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:43.510
Harry Potter books in a single chat. And

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:45.110
when it comes to shorter memory, i.e.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:46.629
the context window, there's two problems

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:48.629
that usually arises. One is the fuller

00:06:48.639 --> 00:06:50.469
that the chat gets, the worse that

00:06:50.479 --> 00:06:52.469
Claude gets at recalling what's inside

00:06:52.479 --> 00:06:54.070
it. And Tropic actually has a name for

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:55.510
this. They call it context rut. You may

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:57.029
have heard of it. And the second problem

00:06:57.039 --> 00:06:59.430
is that everything in that window, every

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:01.189
prompt that you send to Claude, that

00:07:01.199 --> 00:07:03.430
actually gets reread every time. And so

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:05.749
a bloated chat also costs you more and

00:07:05.759 --> 00:07:07.830
drains your usage faster. And so to

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:09.909
address each one, the first tip is to

00:07:09.919 --> 00:07:11.830
compact with a purpose. So when a chat

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:13.670
gets long and you reach that 1 million

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:15.430
token limit, Cloud will automatically

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:17.110
compact your chat, which is essentially

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:19.029
just it's sending a prompt to itself to

00:07:19.039 --> 00:07:21.029
summarize the chat so far. But what's

00:07:21.039 --> 00:07:22.309
important here is that you can actually

00:07:22.319 --> 00:07:23.749
tell it what to keep. Like for example,

00:07:23.759 --> 00:07:25.990
you can type /compact and just keep the

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:27.830
business decisions or the templates that

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:29.430
we've created as part of this chat.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:30.550
Because if you don't do that, then

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:32.390
Claude will just decide what matters and

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.309
it might drop the one detail that you

00:07:34.319 --> 00:07:35.830
might actually need. And if you want to

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:37.670
guide Claude with compacting with a

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:38.870
purpose, meaning you give it some

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:40.390
principles of what to always keep

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:42.230
whenever compacting, then that's just

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:43.990
one simple prompt away and Claude will

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:46.230
probably amend its claw.md to reflect

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:48.150
your wishes. And the second tip with

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:49.589
regard to that rereading every

00:07:49.599 --> 00:07:51.350
conversation, every prompt is to watch

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.430
what we call your cache. And the prompt

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:55.350
cache, that's essentially Claude keeping

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:57.189
what's in your chat in its short-term

00:07:57.199 --> 00:07:58.710
memory. So that when you send a prompt,

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:00.629
when it has to reread that same whole

00:08:00.639 --> 00:08:02.629
conversation, that only costs a small

00:08:02.639 --> 00:08:04.790
fraction of the normal price. But if you

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:06.710
leave a certain chat idle for about an

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.270
hour, that cash goes cold and your next

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.270
message would pay to load that whole

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:12.950
chat all over again. To give you an

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:14.950
example, in one big chat of mine, you

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:16.629
can see here this chat has been idle for

00:08:16.639 --> 00:08:18.550
11 hours and 30 minutes. And if I were

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:20.469
to send a message here, it would reach

00:08:20.479 --> 00:08:23.270
around 600,000 tokens. And given the

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:25.029
model, which is OPUS, that would account

00:08:25.039 --> 00:08:27.830
for around $5 in API credits, which

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.430
right now, since I still have my usage

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:31.990
limits, it will just drain this usage

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:33.829
and by quite a lot. And by the way, this

00:08:33.839 --> 00:08:35.829
is a mod that I created, which if you're

00:08:35.839 --> 00:08:37.430
new, mods are essentially sort of like

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:39.350
Chrome extensions, but for your cloud

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:40.949
code. So, it adds capabilities like

00:08:40.959 --> 00:08:43.029
these that don't cost any tokens. And

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:44.550
you can see in my operating system, I

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:46.310
consider the prompt cache so important

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:48.630
that apart from my 7-day and 5 hour

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:51.350
usage limits, I make sure to always keep

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:53.509
that visible to me. And to give another

00:08:53.519 --> 00:08:55.110
session example, you can see this one,

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:57.110
the prompt cache is still warm. So, even

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:59.190
if I send a message in here, I won't

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:01.910
have to pay the full price of these 186k

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.070
tokens that are currently in this

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:05.750
claude's short-term memory or context

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:07.190
window. And you can see here, this chat

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:09.350
has been idle for around 9 minutes. So,

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:11.590
if I wait for around 51 minutes more,

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:13.670
this whole chat will get cold. And so,

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:15.190
the other thing that I do is I actually

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:17.190
have a directive for each of my sessions

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:20.070
to self-ping themselves for the next 4

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:22.310
hours around every hour. And that way I

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.630
can extend my 1 hour limit of the cash

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:27.350
expiring into 4 hours. And so that's

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:28.870
something that you can do as well. Or

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:30.949
you can also even run an analysis like

00:09:30.959 --> 00:09:32.710
have Claude look at the last 30 days of

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:34.710
your chats, how much would it have saved

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:36.710
